<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.14.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit" Version="2.9.3" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="2.8.2">
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
    </PackageReference>
    <PackageReference Include="coverlet.collector" Version="6.0.4">
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
    </PackageReference>
    <PackageReference Include="Moq" Version="4.20.72" />
    <PackageReference Include="FluentAssertions" Version="6.12.2" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    TEST-ONLY project references, and they are the whole of this project's graph.

    DnnMigration.Application brings Domain with it, so the service, validator, mapper
    and domain suites placed here by AAP 0.4.1.1 compile against everything they need.

    DnnMigration.Infrastructure is referenced for the Security suites, and that
    reference is required rather than convenient. AAP 0.5.1.5 names
    Infrastructure/Security/*.cs as the source of
    Security/{BcryptPasswordHasher,JwtTokenService,PermissionEvaluator}Tests.cs -
    "Hash round-trip, token claims, permission resolution" - and AAP 0.2.1.1 places
    the permission-evaluator tests here. BcryptPasswordHasher and PermissionEvaluator
    are internal sealed, so the owning project grants this assembly - and only this
    assembly - internals visibility; the justification and its bounds are written out
    next to that item in DnnMigration.Infrastructure.csproj. An earlier revision of
    this comment forbade the reference on layering grounds. THAT REASONING WAS WRONG
    and is replaced rather than softened: the layering guarantee is a property of what
    the four PRODUCTION project files reference, and it still holds exactly as before -
    Domain declares zero project references, Application cannot see Infrastructure,
    and either violation remains a compile error. A test project is the outermost
    consumer of the graph, not a layer within it, so nothing about the direction of
    production dependencies changes here. What the old reading did cost was real: the
    two components that hash credentials and decide access were covered only by
    stand-ins, and a review found that the concrete implementations could regress
    while the suite stayed green.

    Do not add DnnMigration.Api. The Api layer is the integration project's subject,
    it is where the hosted pipeline and the authorisation policies live, and a unit
    test that started a host would stop being one.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\DnnMigration.Application\DnnMigration.Application.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\DnnMigration.Infrastructure\DnnMigration.Infrastructure.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
